(D8 — Discrepancy 8 repro: SEGFAULT. Six lines of legal Vox. Two
 string-initialised buffers, plus one buffer received from a function
 whose return expression is a text literal (`Return a buffer, "ABC".`),
 and the program dies before printing anything. Deterministic: exit 139,
 3/3 runs.
 Same construct as Discrepancy 7 (D7.vox), different outcome. With ONE
 string-initialised buffer in the program the call silently yields an
 empty buffer; with TWO or more it segfaults, and the second one counts
 whether it is at top level or inside a function body. Replacing the
 literal return with a buffer-VARIABLE return (`a buffer called made is
 "ABC". Return a buffer, made.`) is safe at any count, so the text
 literal in the return expression is the trigger and the buffer count is
 only what decides whether the damage is visible.
 This is a memory-safety fault on a program that compiles clean, which
 CLAUDE.md rates top severity: Vox promises that no program, however
 stupid, segfaults. Not filed — recorded with the repro, per
 PROCEDURE.md §5.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D8.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   timeout: the monitored command dumped core
   exit 139
)
To 'give literal'. Return a buffer, "ABC".

a buffer called direct is "ABC".
a buffer called second is "DEF".
a buffer called 'from literal' is 'give literal'.
Print 'from literal''s size.
